+	/*
+	 * IMAGE 279 -- the UCHE's address filters, CAF's values.
+	 *
+	 * MEASURED (image 276, a healthy CP):
+	 *   - the CP DOES execute userspace command streams (a CP_EVENT_WRITE
+	 *     from a userspace IB landed in the memptrs page, 0x5A5A1234);
+	 *   - the CP DOES write a userspace buffer (CP_MEM_WRITE 0x11111111
+	 *     landed in the target buffer, A_WROTE=True);
+	 *   - while every shader-domain write still fails (SSBO 0/64, FBO
+	 *     0/6, CP_BLIT 0/4096) with no SMMU fault and a correct mapping.
+	 *
+	 * So the CP's write path is fine and the 3D domain's is not.  The UCHE
+	 * (the shader memory client) has its OWN address filters, and CAF's
+	 * power-up restore list says they belong at ZERO:
+	 *
+	 *   drivers/gpu/msm/adreno_a6xx.c: a6xx_pwrup_reglist[]
+	 *     { A6XX_UCHE_WRITE_RANGE_MAX_LO, 0x0 }, { ..._HI, 0x0 },
+	 *     { A6XX_UCHE_TRAP_BASE_LO, 0x0 },      { ..._HI, 0x0 },
+	 *     { A6XX_UCHE_WRITE_THRU_BASE_LO, 0x0 },{ ..._HI, 0x0 },
+	 *
+	 * We instead write upstream msm's values (a6xx_gpu.c: the "Disable L2
+	 * bypass in the UCHE" block):
+	 *     WRITE_RANGE_MAX = 0x0001ffff_ffffffc0
+	 *     TRAP_BASE       = 0x0001ffff_fffff000
+	 *     WRITE_THRU_BASE = 0x0001ffff_fffff000
+	 *
+	 * The project's audit compared these against UPSTREAM (we match) and
+	 * never against CAF, which is the one driver known to work on this
+	 * exact part under the same TZ.  The register names say exactly what
+	 * they are: a write-range limit and a trap base for the shader client.
+	 * With upstream's values the only writable window is near the top of
+	 * the 49-bit space, while our IOVAs live at 0x700000000.
+	 *
+	 * a6xx.uche_filter_clear=1 writes CAF's zeros (and reads them back);
+	 * default 0 keeps upstream's behaviour.
+	 */
